Good! Wendy's it is. Yeah.

499
00:20:38.481 --> 00:20:39.782
Do you know?
I've never had a Wendy.

500
00:20:41.084 --> 00:20:42.784
(CHUCKLES)
That came out wrong.

501
00:20:42.886 --> 00:20:44.586
-(BARNES AND PAXTON CHUCKLE)
-It's okay.

502
00:20:44.686 --> 00:20:48.491
MR. REED: So when I started
studying theology.

503
00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:50.626
the last thing
I wanted to do was find...

504
00:20:50.726 --> 00:20:53.296
the Wendy's of religions.

505
00:20:53.396 --> 00:20:55.398
I was just writing
a research paper

506
00:20:55.498 --> 00:20:57.867
for a college class. and I was
content with dabbling.

507
00:20:57.966 --> 00:21:00.970
So. some McNuggets here.
BK Whopper over there.

508
00:21:01.071 --> 00:21:03.940
whatever fit the mood.
(INHALES DEEPLY)

509
00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:08.211
As I studied the genres.
McD. BK. In-N-Out.

510
00:21:08.311 --> 00:21:12.514
i.e.. Mormonism. Scientology.
Islam. Buddhism.

511
00:21:12.614 --> 00:21:14.951
as I got closer to God

512
00:21:15.051 --> 00:21:18.988
through genre
and rigorous study.

513
00:21:19.089 --> 00:21:21.590
as I worked
on my personal relationship

514
00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:24.726
with Heavenly Father.
and I think strengthened it.

515
00:21:25.694 --> 00:21:27.297
do you know what I found?

516
00:21:29.065 --> 00:21:32.268
The more you know.
the less you know.

517
00:21:32.368 --> 00:21:33.369
(CANDLE SIZZLING OUT)

518
00:21:33.470 --> 00:21:34.770
(UNSETTLING MUSIC PLAYING)

519
00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:37.307
And by the time I was 50.
I was malnourished

520
00:21:37.407 --> 00:21:40.844
from the fast food of religion
I'd been packing into my brain

521
00:21:40.944 --> 00:21:43.413
for the best part of a decade.

522
00:21:43.513 --> 00:21:46.883
Every sect. cult.
creed. denomination

523
00:21:46.983 --> 00:21:49.152
all claimed to be the one.
true doctrine.

524
00:21:49.252 --> 00:21:52.856
and yet none seemed true when
held under the microscope.

525
00:21:55.591 --> 00:21:57.759
So I wondered
what else was out there.

526
00:21:59.628 --> 00:22:01.397
I promise you.

527
00:22:01.498 --> 00:22:03.1000
the last thing I wanted to do

528
00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:06.236
was find
the one true religion.

529
00:22:09.372 --> 00:22:11.107
But unfortunately. I did.

530
00:22:16.813 --> 00:22:19.182
(UNSETTLING MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

531
00:22:22.485 --> 00:22:24.988
Can we meet your wife?

532
00:22:25.088 --> 00:22:26.156
Please?

533
00:22:26.255 --> 00:22:28.790
MR. REED: Of course. yes!
(CHUCKLES)

534
00:22:28.892 --> 00:22:31.928
She just has to be present.
and we want

535
00:22:32.028 --> 00:22:34.497
-to meet her as well.
-BARNES: Yeah.

536
00:22:34.597 --> 00:22:36.665
Sorry. I'll... I'll go ask.

537
00:22:42.338 --> 00:22:43.873
(TIMER WINDING)

538
00:22:45.875 --> 00:22:47.743
(DOOR CREAKS)

539
00:22:47.844 --> 00:22:49.913
(FOOTSTEPS RECEDING)

540
00:22:55.284 --> 00:22:56.553
(DOOR OPENS)

541
00:22:56.653 --> 00:22:58.288
(LIGHT BUZZING)

542
00:22:58.388 --> 00:22:59.822
(CLOCK TICKING)

543
00:22:59.923 --> 00:23:02.258
PAXTON: Sister Barnes.
I don't... I don't know.

544
00:23:03.993 --> 00:23:05.328
Yeah.
I don't want to spook him.

545
00:23:05.428 --> 00:23:06.930
but we should wrap this.

546
00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:09.199
Any messages?

547
00:23:10.832 --> 00:23:13.269
-Nothing.
-(EXHALES)

548
00:23:13.369 --> 00:23:15.171
Okay. Um...

549
00:23:15.271 --> 00:23:17.874
let's just politely wrap this
and head back.

550
00:23:18.707 --> 00:23:20.210
PAXTON:
Just the highlights...

551
00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:22.312
(CRACKLING)

552
00:23:26.482 --> 00:23:29.284
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

553
00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:36.459
(SCRAPING)

554
00:23:44.701 --> 00:23:46.135
(GASPS)

555
00:23:49.305 --> 00:23:51.241
(CLOCK TICKING)

556
00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:56.778
-(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)
-(CLOCK TICKING INTENSIFIES)

557
00:23:58.380 --> 00:23:59.549
-(DOOR OPENS)
-(MUSIC AND TICKING STOPS)

558
00:23:59.649 --> 00:24:01.951
MR. REED:
And she is finally ready.

559
00:24:02.051 --> 00:24:03.820
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

560
00:24:03.919 --> 00:24:05.455
(CHUCKLES) Sorry about that.

561
00:24:07.389 --> 00:24:10.659
(EXHALES) Come on through.
She's eager to... meet you.

562
00:24:10.759 --> 00:24:12.028
There we are.

563
00:24:12.761 --> 00:24:14.430
-This way.
-Okay.

564
00:24:15.365 --> 00:24:17.533
(GLASSES CLINKING)

565
00:24:22.972 --> 00:24:25.675
One moment. Mr. Reed.
Uh. we're just getting a call

566
00:24:25.774 --> 00:24:27.310
from the Relief Society
president.

567
00:24:27.409 --> 00:24:28.645
-We'll be right there.
-Oh. do you need a minute?

568
00:24:28.745 --> 00:24:29.846
Hi.

569
00:24:29.946 --> 00:24:31.180
-This is Sister Barnes. Um...
-I'll wait.

570
00:24:31.281 --> 00:24:32.682
-I'll wait on the other side.
-Yeah. Just one moment.

571
00:24:32.781 --> 00:24:33.983
-Thank you.
-Yeah.

572
00:24:40.623 --> 00:24:42.524
(DOOR CREAKS. SHUTS)

573
00:24:46.029 --> 00:24:47.730
-(SOFTLY) Should we leave?
-(SOFTLY) Now?

574
00:24:47.864 --> 00:24:50.300
-(SOFTLY) Yeah?
-(SOFTLY) Yes? Yes.

575
00:24:50.399 --> 00:24:51.634
(EXHALES)

576
00:24:51.734 --> 00:24:53.836
-(FLOOR CREAKING)
-(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

577
00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:00.576
(TENSE MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

578
00:25:01.778 --> 00:25:02.979
What is it?

579
00:25:03.513 --> 00:25:04.747
Our bikes.

580
00:25:04.847 --> 00:25:06.115
Yeah?

581
00:25:06.582 --> 00:25:07.582
They're locked outside.

582
00:25:07.684 --> 00:25:08.885
So what?

583
00:25:09.552 --> 00:25:10.919
He has our coats.

584
00:25:12.088 --> 00:25:14.023
The key to the bike lock
is in my coat pocket.

585
00:25:14.123 --> 00:25:15.658
So we need our coats.

586
00:25:15.758 --> 00:25:17.527
Yeah.

587
00:25:17.627 --> 00:25:19.762
-We do.
-I don't know. do we?

588
00:25:19.861 --> 00:25:20.997
Walk back
without coats and bikes?

589
00:25:21.097 --> 00:25:22.865
What is that. two miles?

590
00:25:22.965 --> 00:25:25.101
No. I think it's four.

591
00:25:25.201 --> 00:25:26.668
It's a little over the top.
but I...

592
00:25:26.769 --> 00:25:28.438
I'm fine.
I'm fine to walk. yeah?

593
00:25:28.538 --> 00:25:30.940
(DOORKNOB RATTLING)

594
00:25:32.975 --> 00:25:34.711
-It's stuck.
-Unlock it.

595
00:25:34.811 --> 00:25:36.479
(TENSE MUSIC BUILDS)

596
00:25:36.579 --> 00:25:39.048
(WHISPERS) There's no... lock.

597
00:25:39.148 --> 00:25:40.616
BARNES: (WHISPERING)
What? Pull harder.

598
00:25:40.716 --> 00:25:42.452
Here. Here.

599
00:25:42.552 --> 00:25:44.520
(DOORKNOB RATTLING)

600
00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:45.688
BARNES: All right.

601
00:25:45.788 --> 00:25:47.523
(RATTLING)

602
00:25:47.623 --> 00:25:49.425
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

603
00:25:49.524 --> 00:25:51.928
(PAXTON BREATHING HEAVILY)

604
00:25:53.029 --> 00:25:54.530
(BARNES GRUNTING)

605
00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:56.032
-(CREAKING)
-(DOOR CLOSES)

606
00:25:56.132 --> 00:25:58.334
-(SHUSHES)
-(MUSIC STOPS PLAYING)

607
00:25:58.434 --> 00:26:00.303
(DISTANT FOOTSTEPS)

608
00:26:00.403 --> 00:26:01.870
(PAXTON SHUDDERS)

609
00:26:07.342 --> 00:26:08.945
BARNES: (WHISPERING)
Is he still there?

610
00:26:10.012 --> 00:26:11.781
PAXTON: (WHISPERING)
Didn't we hear him leave?

611
00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:13.182
BARNES: Are you sure?

612
00:26:14.818 --> 00:26:16.319
Should we ask him for help?

613
00:26:16.418 --> 00:26:17.486
No.

614
00:26:19.555 --> 00:26:20.556
(EXHALES SHARPLY)

615
00:26:21.991 --> 00:26:22.992
Can you fit through this?

616
00:26:23.091 --> 00:26:24.627
-PAXTON: You crazy?
-(SIGHS)

617
00:26:24.726 --> 00:26:25.695
(WIND BLOWING)

618
00:26:25.794 --> 00:26:28.164
Um. we should try Sister Hall.
okay?

619
00:26:29.532 --> 00:26:32.735
Or Elder Kennedy. just so
they know where we are.

620
00:26:32.836 --> 00:26:34.270
-(BARNES SIGHS HEAVILY)
-(PHONE BEEPS)

621
00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:35.171
(LINE RINGING)

622
00:26:35.271 --> 00:26:37.607
-(WIND GUSTING)
-(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

623
00:26:44.614 --> 00:26:45.380
(LINE BEEPING)

624
00:26:45.481 --> 00:26:47.582
(SIGHS)
It's not going through.

625
00:26:49.118 --> 00:26:50.919
It's the storm.
Keep trying. okay?

626
00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:52.188
(PHONE BEEPS)

627
00:26:52.288 --> 00:26:54.858
-(BREATH TREMBLES)
-(LINE RINGING)

628
00:26:54.957 --> 00:26:57.260
-(DOORKNOB RATTLING)
-(GRUNTS)

629
00:26:57.927 --> 00:26:59.762
(PANTING)

630
00:26:59.863 --> 00:27:01.431
(DOOR CREAKING)

631
00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:07.905
(WIND GUSTING)

632
00:27:08.004 --> 00:27:09.372
-(DOORKNOB RATTLES)
-(PANTING)

633
00:27:09.472 --> 00:27:11.674
-(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)
-(BREATH TREMBLING)

634
00:27:16.546 --> 00:27:17.513
(EXHALES)

635
00:27:17.613 --> 00:27:18.581
Okay.

636
00:27:20.716 --> 00:27:22.185
We ask for help. hmm?

637
00:27:24.687 --> 00:27:25.788
It'll be fine.

638
00:27:25.889 --> 00:27:28.157
(OMINOUS CREAKING)

639
00:27:29.992 --> 00:27:31.829
(RAPID TICKING)

640
00:27:31.928 --> 00:27:33.729
-(LIGHTS CLICK)
-(GASPS)

641
00:27:39.903 --> 00:27:41.704
(FOOTSTEPS)

642
00:27:41.803 --> 00:27:42.805
(TIMER WINDING)

643
00:27:42.906 --> 00:27:44.106
(CRANKING)

644
00:27:49.947 --> 00:27:51.882
(FLOOR CREAKING LIGHTLY)

645
00:27:51.981 --> 00:27:54.183
(BREATH TREMBLING)

646
00:28:05.863 --> 00:28:08.030
(FLOOR CREAKING LIGHTLY)

647
00:28:10.834 --> 00:28:11.869
(FLOOR CREAKING)

648
00:28:11.969 --> 00:28:14.036
(BOTH BREATHING HEAVILY)

649
00:28:17.773 --> 00:28:19.140
PAXTON: Mr. Reed?

650
00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:39.428
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

651
00:28:45.101 --> 00:28:46.502
(MUSIC FADES OUT)

652
00:28:46.601 --> 00:28:48.704
(SIGHS. CHUCKLES)

653
00:28:55.311 --> 00:28:56.712
(DOOR LOCK CLICKS)

654
00:28:56.813 --> 00:28:59.282
<i>(JUST LIKE A BUTTERFLY THAT'S</i>
<i>CAUGHT IN THE RAIN</i> PLAYING)

655
00:29:00.582 --> 00:29:02.685
(DOOR CREAKS)

656
00:29:19.335 --> 00:29:21.169
And there they are! Welcome.

657
00:29:22.538 --> 00:29:25.374
Mr. Reed. you have been
too generous with your time.

658
00:29:25.474 --> 00:29:27.243
Sister Paxton and I
were just saying

659
00:29:27.342 --> 00:29:28.644
what a pleasure it's been.

660
00:29:28.744 --> 00:29:31.414
Um. but we just got off
the phone with Sister Hall.

661
00:29:31.514 --> 00:29:33.749
and she needs us
to return to the church.

662
00:29:33.850 --> 00:29:36.887
-MR. REED: Oh! Oh. well...
-Unfortunately. yeah.

663
00:29:36.986 --> 00:29:39.856
I won't keep you
if you wish to leave.

664
00:29:39.957 --> 00:29:42.291
We've been summoned back.

665
00:29:42.792 --> 00:29:44.427
Yeah.

666
00:29:44.527 --> 00:29:45.928
Damn it! Damn it.
'cause we were just

667
00:29:46.029 --> 00:29:47.229
getting started. weren't we?

668
00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:49.332
You know. you had me thinking
that maybe you were

669
00:29:49.432 --> 00:29:52.069
onto something
with your visit. (CHUCKLES)

670
00:29:52.168 --> 00:29:55.236
I've been impressed.
but I understand.

671
00:29:56.138 --> 00:29:57.306
Uh. yeah. your coats are here.

672
00:29:57.406 --> 00:29:58.875
hanging on the back
of the pew.

673
00:29:58.976 --> 00:30:00.810
Excuse me one moment.

674
00:30:00.911 --> 00:30:02.311
Yeah.

675
00:30:02.411 --> 00:30:03.813
<i>(JUST LIKE A BUTTERFLY THAT'S</i>
<i>CAUGHT IN THE RAIN</i> PLAYING)

676
00:30:15.558 --> 00:30:16.659
(THUD)

677
00:30:19.863 --> 00:30:21.898
(WATER SPLASHING)

678
00:30:33.976 --> 00:30:35.311
(MOUTHING) Key.

679
00:30:53.063 --> 00:30:55.665
So... so we just. uh.
need your help

680
00:30:55.764 --> 00:30:56.899
with the door. Um...

681
00:30:56.1000 --> 00:30:58.935
It's...it's a little tricky.

682
00:30:59.036 --> 00:31:00.269
I'm sorry?

683
00:31:00.369 --> 00:31:02.772
PAXTON: I think your door is
a bit stuck out there.

684
00:31:03.205 --> 00:31:04.473
The door won't open.

685
00:31:05.441 --> 00:31:06.676
PAXTON:
The front door.

686
00:31:06.776 --> 00:31:08.611
Yeah. the front door
won't open again.

687
00:31:09.478 --> 00:31:11.213
It opened when we came in.

688
00:31:11.313 --> 00:31:13.015
-(CHUCKLES NERVOUSLY)
-MR. REED: Yeah. (CHUCKLES)

689
00:31:13.116 --> 00:31:14.784
Maybe if you unlock it.
it will open?

690
00:31:14.885 --> 00:31:16.986
No. no. I... I... I understand
what you're asking me.

691
00:31:17.087 --> 00:31:18.553
but. uh.

692
00:31:19.122 --> 00:31:21.322
the deadbolts are on a timer.

693
00:31:21.424 --> 00:31:23.591
I got carried away
with our conversation.

694
00:31:23.693 --> 00:31:25.494
I didn't realize the brace
had been set.

695
00:31:25.594 --> 00:31:27.563
I should have pulled the pin
when you came in.

696
00:31:27.663 --> 00:31:30.167
but I forgot.
So if you are now

697
00:31:30.265 --> 00:31:32.234
regrettably ready to leave.

698
00:31:32.334 --> 00:31:34.203
you'll have to exit
through the back of my house.

699
00:31:36.173 --> 00:31:37.841
Uh. where?

700
00:31:37.941 --> 00:31:39.274
Just through here.

701
00:31:41.476 --> 00:31:44.114
Well. can you just unlock
the front one. please?

702
00:31:44.213 --> 00:31:46.149
We would like to go that way.

703
00:31:46.248 --> 00:31:48.250
Just so we don't
get turned around

704
00:31:48.350 --> 00:31:50.586
and confused
when we get outside.

705
00:31:50.686 --> 00:31:53.889
Yeah. it won't open again
'til morning.

706
00:31:57.094 --> 00:31:58.962
(MUSIC STOPS PLAYING)

707
00:31:59.062 --> 00:32:00.696
Please. sir?

708
00:32:00.796 --> 00:32:01.664
MR. REED: Oh. I'm sorry.

709
00:32:01.764 --> 00:32:04.533
I promise you.
it's safe to leave this way.

710
00:32:04.633 --> 00:32:05.701
Huh?

711
00:32:09.438 --> 00:32:12.341
It's a little unusual
to have a door

712
00:32:12.441 --> 00:32:15.745
that. um. locks on a timer?

713
00:32:15.846 --> 00:32:16.847
MR. REED: Uh...

714
00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:18.949
Yes. well. I see that.

715
00:32:19.049 --> 00:32:20.884
(CHUCKLES) Yeah.

716
00:32:20.984 --> 00:32:22.085
And it's...

717
00:32:22.185 --> 00:32:25.088
Well. it's a little unusual
that it can't be unlocked.

718
00:32:25.188 --> 00:32:26.555
MR. REED: No.
I... I... I know.

719
00:32:26.655 --> 00:32:30.392
I am self-aware enough to know
how this might look.

720
00:32:30.493 --> 00:32:33.196
Older man. two young women
in his house.

721
00:32:33.294 --> 00:32:35.932
but I assure you.
you are welcome to leave

722
00:32:36.032 --> 00:32:37.134
through...through the back.

723
00:32:37.234 --> 00:32:38.567
You saw my house
from the outside. right?

724
00:32:38.667 --> 00:32:39.668
BARNES: Mm-hmm.
MR. REED: Right!

725
00:32:39.769 --> 00:32:40.770
So. you know
it's a tiny footprint.

726
00:32:40.870 --> 00:32:42.705
The back of the house
is just there.

727
00:32:42.805 --> 00:32:43.874
(INHALES) Yeah.

728
00:32:45.841 --> 00:32:49.578
Anyway. I will leave you space
to make your decision freely.

729
00:32:49.678 --> 00:32:50.646
Yeah.

730
00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:03.193
To clarify. I am trying...

731
00:33:03.292 --> 00:33:05.829
have been trying very hard
to make it a point

732
00:33:05.928 --> 00:33:06.695
not to pressure you.

733
00:33:06.796 --> 00:33:08.397
so you shouldn't even
feel the need

734
00:33:08.497 --> 00:33:11.667
to fabricate a story
about someone from your ward

735
00:33:11.767 --> 00:33:14.503
calling you to leave.
It's... perfectly fine.

736
00:33:14.603 --> 00:33:16.273
Leave when you want.

737
00:33:16.372 --> 00:33:19.575
It wasn't an excuse.
they do need us back.

738
00:33:19.675 --> 00:33:21.476
No. I know. but you're saying
you took a call

739
00:33:21.577 --> 00:33:22.778
with someone from your ward.

740
00:33:22.879 --> 00:33:24.547
Yeah. a moment ago.

741
00:33:24.647 --> 00:33:26.315
But when you came inside.
I asked if you were okay

742
00:33:26.415 --> 00:33:28.317
with the metal in the walls
and ceilings.

743
00:33:28.417 --> 00:33:30.053
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

744
00:33:30.153 --> 00:33:31.988
What do you mean?

745
00:33:32.088 --> 00:33:34.623
I mean that the metal
obstructs cell phone signals.

746
00:33:34.723 --> 00:33:37.092
so I do know
you didn't take a call. But...

747
00:33:37.194 --> 00:33:39.229
(CHUCKLES) Just to be clear.

748
00:33:39.328 --> 00:33:40.596
so you're comfortable.

749
00:33:40.696 --> 00:33:43.899
it is totally fine for you
to leave on your own accord.

750
00:33:43.1000 --> 00:33:46.837
You never need to feel like
you have to... (SIGHS)

751
00:33:46.937 --> 00:33:49.605
...make excuses or...
or tell me little...

752
00:33:49.705 --> 00:33:51.574
little... little white lies.

753
00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:54.945
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

754
00:33:58.681 --> 00:34:02.853
So which door
takes us outside?

755
00:34:02.953 --> 00:34:04.287
MR. REED: Pff.

756
00:34:04.386 --> 00:34:06.322
Well... (CHUCKLES)
do you have a preference?

757
00:34:06.422 --> 00:34:09.358
Wh... why would I
have a preference?

758
00:34:09.457 --> 00:34:11.995
PAXTON: Should we have
a preference?

759
00:34:12.095 --> 00:34:13.729
That is a fair question.

760
00:34:15.798 --> 00:34:17.666
Not to be completely weird.
but can your wife

761
00:34:17.765 --> 00:34:20.502
please just step in the room.

762
00:34:20.602 --> 00:34:22.604
say hello and walk us
to the back of the house?

763
00:34:22.705 --> 00:34:24.140
I'm not trying to be silly.

764
00:34:24.240 --> 00:34:25.841
I promise. I'm really not.

765
00:34:25.941 --> 00:34:29.778
but there are rules we follow.
and we were upfront with them.

766
00:34:31.214 --> 00:34:32.481
I will ask.

767
00:34:40.789 --> 00:34:43.360
Can I ask you
a question first?

768
00:34:43.459 --> 00:34:45.628
(TENSE MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

769
00:34:47.495 --> 00:34:49.032
Do you...

770
00:34:51.034 --> 00:34:53.870
still believe that my wife
is in the next room?

771
00:34:55.005 --> 00:34:57.874
Despite all the evidence
to the contrary.

772
00:34:57.974 --> 00:34:59.376
the scented candle.

773
00:34:59.475 --> 00:35:02.644
the absence of an oven
with blueberry pie?

774
00:35:03.611 --> 00:35:06.917
Or have you been politely
indulging a lie?

775
00:35:07.017 --> 00:35:09.285
If you still believe that
she's in there. I'll go ask.

776
00:35:09.385 --> 00:35:11.452
but it's something
I want you to think about

777
00:35:11.553 --> 00:35:12.755
and maybe think about

778
00:35:12.856 --> 00:35:15.258
in the context
of your beliefs.

779
00:35:15.358 --> 00:35:16.591
(FOOTSTEPS)

780
00:35:16.691 --> 00:35:18.427
Do you believe in God because

781
00:35:18.526 --> 00:35:20.930
somebody told you
at an impressionable age

782
00:35:21.031 --> 00:35:23.731
that God is real.
despite having doubts

783
00:35:23.832 --> 00:35:26.102
as you got older. despite...

784
00:35:26.202 --> 00:35:28.405
seeing evidence to
the contrary your whole lives?

785
00:35:28.504 --> 00:35:30.106
When your father
lost control of his body.

786
00:35:30.206 --> 00:35:33.742
did you think it was
God's plan to ruin his life?

787
00:35:33.843 --> 00:35:35.443
Or did you go on
believing something

788
00:35:35.544 --> 00:35:38.148
that you know is not true
just to give you comfort

789
00:35:38.247 --> 00:35:40.082
because you were afraid
of what it might mean

790
00:35:40.182 --> 00:35:42.084
if it was all a lie?

791
00:35:43.954 --> 00:35:46.289
I put the scented candle
on the table

792
00:35:46.389 --> 00:35:48.291
because I wanted you to think

793
00:35:48.390 --> 00:35:49.591
about the things
that you believe

794
00:35:49.691 --> 00:35:52.963
just because somebody
asked you to believe them.

795
00:35:57.800 --> 00:35:58.801
Can...

796
00:36:00.437 --> 00:36:01.736
Can we use your phone?

797
00:36:02.272 --> 00:36:03.472
Please?

798
00:36:04.473 --> 00:36:06.542
I... I don't have a telephone.

799
00:36:15.517 --> 00:36:19.588
You have a really
beautiful home. Mr. Reed.

800
00:36:19.688 --> 00:36:21.657
It's like a church in here.

801
00:36:21.756 --> 00:36:23.059
Did you build it yourself?

802
00:36:24.193 --> 00:36:25.294
My wife built it.

803
00:36:25.794 --> 00:36:27.629
-(WATER POURING)
-(CLACK)

804
00:36:27.729 --> 00:36:29.899
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

805
00:36:34.503 --> 00:36:36.305
We're gonna leave

806
00:36:36.406 --> 00:36:38.274
through the back
of your house.

807
00:36:38.374 --> 00:36:39.376
Now. is that okay?

808
00:36:39.476 --> 00:36:40.642
MR. REED: Of course.

809
00:36:42.678 --> 00:36:43.711
BARNES: Just like you said.
it's okay.

810
00:36:43.813 --> 00:36:46.715
and we can leave. right?

811
00:36:46.816 --> 00:36:48.118
MR. REED: That's right.

812
00:36:48.550 --> 00:36:50.186
(TENSE MUSIC BUILDS)

813
00:37:05.068 --> 00:37:06.936
(DOOR CREAKING)

814
00:37:07.037 --> 00:37:09.171
(MUSIC FADES OUT)

815
00:37:12.142 --> 00:37:13.176
What is it?

816
00:37:13.276 --> 00:37:15.044
(OMINOUS MUSIC RISES)

817
00:37:21.950 --> 00:37:23.486
-(DOOR CLOSES)
-(MUSIC FADES OUT)

818
00:37:23.584 --> 00:37:26.055
-(BREATHING HEAVILY)
-Sister Barnes?

819
00:37:35.131 --> 00:37:37.167
(DOOR CREAKING)

820
00:37:41.137 --> 00:37:42.338
That's a basement.

821
00:37:42.439 --> 00:37:44.640
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

822
00:37:45.740 --> 00:37:48.343
It doesn't look like
this goes outside.

823
00:37:53.649 --> 00:37:55.818
(WIND GUSTING)

824
00:37:59.456 --> 00:38:01.356
(SLOW. SUSPENSEFUL MUSIC
PLAYING)

825
00:38:10.666 --> 00:38:12.836
(WIND GUSTING)

826
00:38:17.474 --> 00:38:19.740
(TREE BRANCHES CREAKING)

827
00:38:20.642 --> 00:38:22.611
(BUCKET RATTLING)

828
00:38:23.446 --> 00:38:24.646
(SIGHS)

829
00:38:24.745 --> 00:38:26.715
(WATER RUNNING)

830
00:38:32.421 --> 00:38:34.756
(SUSPENSEFUL MUSIC PLAYING)

831
00:38:40.430 --> 00:38:42.131
(WIND HOWLING)

832
00:38:43.298 --> 00:38:47.336
I think that you can
tell that we're regrettably

833
00:38:47.437 --> 00:38:50.173
uncomfortable with this
situation here tonight.

834
00:38:50.273 --> 00:38:51.640
(CLICK)

835
00:38:51.740 --> 00:38:54.443
I'm sorry. but didn't you just
say a moment ago

836
00:38:54.544 --> 00:38:56.979
that you saw the outside
of my house?

837
00:38:57.080 --> 00:38:59.182
-So then you clearly saw...
-(DOOR CREAKING)

838
00:38:59.282 --> 00:39:01.950
...that the back of my house
overhangs a hill.

839
00:39:02.051 --> 00:39:05.088
so you would know
that you have to go down...

840
00:39:05.188 --> 00:39:06.787
(SHUDDERS)

841
00:39:06.890 --> 00:39:08.524
...in order to go out.

842
00:39:08.624 --> 00:39:09.691
(CHUCKLING)

843
00:39:09.791 --> 00:39:10.894
(DOOR CLOSES)

844
00:39:10.994 --> 00:39:12.727
BARNES: I think
it would be best for you

845
00:39:12.829 --> 00:39:15.398
and for all of us
if you could help us

846
00:39:15.497 --> 00:39:17.633
go home because

847
00:39:17.733 --> 00:39:19.903
the Stake President.
he knows where we are.

848
00:39:20.003 --> 00:39:21.504
And the Ward
Missionaries know.

849
00:39:21.604 --> 00:39:25.408
And they'll be expecting us.

850
00:39:25.508 --> 00:39:28.044
And there were witnesses.
weren't there. Sister Barnes?

851
00:39:28.144 --> 00:39:29.412
On our way here.
we passed a police officer?

852
00:39:29.512 --> 00:39:31.680
MR. REED: Whoa! The police?
(CHUCKLES) I promise you.

853
00:39:31.780 --> 00:39:34.551
you're getting way. way. way.
way too worked up.

854
00:39:34.651 --> 00:39:35.885
BARNES: Right.

855
00:39:35.985 --> 00:39:37.320
But I think that we're scared

856
00:39:37.419 --> 00:39:40.223
because you lied
to us earlier.

857
00:39:40.323 --> 00:39:41.423
Do you want me to help?

858
00:39:41.524 --> 00:39:43.691
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

859
00:39:44.693 --> 00:39:45.693
You asked me earlier
if you should

860
00:39:45.794 --> 00:39:47.197
have a preference
between the doors.

861
00:39:47.296 --> 00:39:49.631
I think you should.
I think it's something

862
00:39:49.765 --> 00:39:51.133
you should consider
very carefully.

863
00:39:51.234 --> 00:39:53.503
and then make
the right decision.

864
00:39:53.602 --> 00:39:55.872
Do you want me to help you?

865
00:39:59.676 --> 00:40:00.776
BARNES: Yes.

866
00:40:00.877 --> 00:40:02.077
MR. REED: Okay.

867
00:40:02.178 --> 00:40:03.213
Then. please...

868
00:40:04.948 --> 00:40:06.983
come and stand here.
Both of you come.

869
00:40:08.551 --> 00:40:09.751
Just here.

870
00:40:09.852 --> 00:40:11.086
That's it.

871
00:40:12.155 --> 00:40:14.224
-It's a simple choice.
-(DOOR CREAKING)

872
00:40:14.323 --> 00:40:15.325
(DOOR CLOSES)

873
00:40:16.292 --> 00:40:18.695
But it should not
be made simply.

874
00:40:18.794 --> 00:40:20.897
(TENSE MUSIC
CONTINUES PLAYING)

875
00:40:21.831 --> 00:40:23.832
I need you to reflect
very. very deeply

876
00:40:23.932 --> 00:40:26.635
and then make
a sincere decision.

877
00:40:26.735 --> 00:40:28.870
(TENSE MUSIC BUILDS)

878
00:40:33.476 --> 00:40:34.776
(MUSIC STOPS PLAYING)

879
00:40:36.379 --> 00:40:37.146
Have you ever played

880
00:40:37.247 --> 00:40:39.781
the Parker Brothers game.
<i>Monopoly?</i>

881
00:40:42.151 --> 00:40:46.055
<i>Monopoly</i> is currently
published in 47 languages.

882
00:40:46.155 --> 00:40:49.926
They sell it
in over 114 countries.

883
00:40:51.294 --> 00:40:52.995
(STATIC)

884
00:40:54.097 --> 00:40:56.466
<i>(THE AIR THAT I BREATHE</i>
BY THE HOLLIES PLAYING)

885
00:41:07.943 --> 00:41:12.282
♪ <i>If I could make a wish ♪</i>

886
00:41:12.381 --> 00:41:15.351
They say over a billion people
have played Monopoly.

887
00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:18.620
But I'm guessing that only
a fraction of that billion

888
00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:19.988
have actually finished it.

889
00:41:20.689 --> 00:41:23.325
Maybe that reminds you

890
00:41:23.426 --> 00:41:24.727
of something else?

891
00:41:24.827 --> 00:41:26.995
Just a thought.

892
00:41:27.095 --> 00:41:30.899
This is what most people mean
when they hear <i>"Monopoly."</i>

893
00:41:30.999 --> 00:41:33.035
There's the pewter avatars.

894
00:41:33.135 --> 00:41:36.005
Everyone has their favorite.
I shall not tell you mine.

895
00:41:36.104 --> 00:41:37.039
Meow!

896
00:41:37.140 --> 00:41:40.008
There's the pastel money
in various denominations.

897
00:41:40.109 --> 00:41:42.644
and. of course. the jail.

898
00:41:42.745 --> 00:41:46.048
Now. this next part
is very important.

899
00:41:46.149 --> 00:41:49.952
Have you ever played
the 1904 board game called

900
00:41:50.053 --> 00:41:52.822
<i>The Landlord's Game?</i>

901
00:41:52.921 --> 00:41:55.925
It is very nearly identical
to <i>Monopoly</i>

902
00:41:56.024 --> 00:41:58.026
in every fundamental way.
There's the jail.

903
00:41:58.127 --> 00:41:59.861
There's free parking.

904
00:41:59.961 --> 00:42:01.464
There's fines and fees.
There's penalties.

905
00:42:01.564 --> 00:42:03.032
for landing
on other people's properties.

906
00:42:03.131 --> 00:42:05.034
and the ultimate goal
of forming monopolies

907
00:42:05.133 --> 00:42:07.903
to force your opponent
out of the game.

908
00:42:08.004 --> 00:42:10.372
What do games have to do
with us leaving?

909
00:42:11.774 --> 00:42:12.975
Everything.

910
00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:16.678
<i>The Landlord's Game</i>
was designed by

911
00:42:16.778 --> 00:42:18.581
American feminist
Elizabeth Magie.

912
00:42:18.680 --> 00:42:20.283
almost three decades before

913
00:42:20.383 --> 00:42:22.718
a heater salesman
from Philadelphia.

914
00:42:22.820 --> 00:42:25.088
Sister Barnes.
changed the name to Monopoly

915
00:42:25.188 --> 00:42:27.523
and sold the concept
as his own

916
00:42:27.622 --> 00:42:29.191
to Parker Brothers in 1935.

917
00:42:29.292 --> 00:42:30.626
This chap was called
Charles Darrow.

918
00:42:30.727 --> 00:42:33.028
went on to become the first
millionaire games designer

919
00:42:33.129 --> 00:42:35.531
in the history of the world.
while poor old Elizabeth Magie

920
00:42:35.631 --> 00:42:37.800
died without ever
being credited for the impact

921
00:42:37.899 --> 00:42:40.303
she had on American culture.

922
00:42:40.403 --> 00:42:41.637
(RECORD SCRATCHES)

923
00:42:43.005 --> 00:42:45.006
<i>(THE AIR THAT I BREATHE</i>
FADES OUT)

924
00:42:45.108 --> 00:42:46.909
I'm talking to you

925
00:42:47.810 --> 00:42:49.178
about iterations.

926
00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:53.249
I need you to have a very
basic understanding

927
00:42:53.349 --> 00:42:56.252
of iterating.
because I'm going to make

928
00:42:56.352 --> 00:42:57.887
a very disturbing
claim tonight.

929
00:42:57.986 --> 00:42:59.889
(TENSE MUSIC PLAYING)

930
00:43:01.124 --> 00:43:03.959
It will make your stomachs
sink a little

931
00:43:04.060 --> 00:43:06.428
and your hearts beat faster.

932
00:43:06.528 --> 00:43:09.867
It will make you sick.

933
00:43:09.999 --> 00:43:11.801
It may even...
I'm very sorry...

934
00:43:11.901 --> 00:43:14.169
make you want to...
to die.

935
00:43:17.707 --> 00:43:20.042
(FOOTSTEPS)

936
00:43:27.851 --> 00:43:29.017
(STATIC)

937
00:43:29.118 --> 00:43:30.919
<i>(THE AIR THAT I BREATHE</i>
BY THE HOLLIES PLAYING)

938
00:43:41.397 --> 00:43:43.166
Have you heard
this song before?

939
00:43:44.801 --> 00:43:46.702
You just played it.

940
00:43:46.803 --> 00:43:49.305
No. I mean before tonight.

941
00:43:49.405 --> 00:43:50.372
PAXTON: Mmm-mmm.

942
00:43:50.908 --> 00:43:52.041
I disagree!

943
00:43:52.140 --> 00:43:54.010
I think you've heard it
many. many times before.

944
00:43:54.110 --> 00:43:56.278
but we will come back to that.

945
00:43:56.379 --> 00:43:59.348
You are. are you not.
monotheistic?

946
00:43:59.448 --> 00:44:01.716
We believe in Heavenly Father.
yes.

947
00:44:01.818 --> 00:44:02.919
MR. REED: Okay.

948
00:44:03.019 --> 00:44:05.787
There are three major
monotheistic religions.

949
00:44:05.889 --> 00:44:09.860
Judaism. Christianity
and Islam.

950
00:44:09.959 --> 00:44:12.128
I call them. "The Big Three."

951
00:44:13.763 --> 00:44:15.765
Judaism. i.e.

952
00:44:15.865 --> 00:44:17.533
"The Original Edition."

953
00:44:17.633 --> 00:44:20.870
Christianity. i.e.
"The Most Popular Edition."

954
00:44:20.970 --> 00:44:24.372
and Islam. "The Newer.

955
00:44:24.908 --> 00:44:26.709
"Second Most Popular Edition."

956
00:44:28.077 --> 00:44:30.713
May I see your Book of Mormon.
please?

957
00:44:32.113 --> 00:44:33.316
I'll give it back.

958
00:44:35.051 --> 00:44:36.052
Thank you.

959
00:44:36.151 --> 00:44:40.456
And finally. after 800 years.
this!

960
00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:44.193
Mormonism. i.e.

961
00:44:45.193 --> 00:44:47.563
"The zany regional
spin-off edition."

962
00:44:52.870 --> 00:44:55.839
These are all iterations

963
00:44:55.939 --> 00:44:57.673
of the same source material.

964
00:44:58.474 --> 00:45:02.178
These texts share
many of the same characters

965
00:45:02.278 --> 00:45:03.546
and histories albeit presented

966
00:45:03.646 --> 00:45:05.680
with different meanings
and perspectives.

967
00:45:06.381 --> 00:45:08.284
So no.

968
00:45:08.383 --> 00:45:10.385
I will not accept... (CHUCKLES)
...that you stand there

969
00:45:10.486 --> 00:45:11.889
and tell me
that you've never heard

970
00:45:11.988 --> 00:45:13.723
<i>The Air That I Breathe</i>
by The Hollies.

971
00:45:13.823 --> 00:45:17.426
when I know that you have
heard <i>Creep</i> by Radiohead.

972
00:45:18.693 --> 00:45:20.831
Oh. yes. you have. Come on.

973
00:45:20.930 --> 00:45:23.265
(SINGING)
♪ <i>But I'm a creep! ♪</i>

974
00:45:23.365 --> 00:45:24.499
Yeah?

975
00:45:24.600 --> 00:45:25.869
♪ <i>I'm a weirdo ♪</i>

976
00:45:25.968 --> 00:45:27.102
(LAUGHING)

977
00:45:27.202 --> 00:45:29.907
Okay! "What the hell
am I doing here?"

978
00:45:30.006 --> 00:45:31.474
(MR. REED LAUGHING)

979
00:45:31.573 --> 00:45:32.943
I know these are things
you are thinking right now.

980
00:45:33.042 --> 00:45:35.311
but they're also lyrics
you recognize. yes?

981
00:45:35.879 --> 00:45:37.213
Yes?

982
00:45:37.313 --> 00:45:39.983
The Hollies filed a plagiarism
lawsuit against Radiohead.

983
00:45:40.082 --> 00:45:41.350
which they later settled
by proving

984
00:45:41.450 --> 00:45:44.120
that the melody and rhythm
of <i>The Air That I Breathe</i>

985
00:45:44.220 --> 00:45:45.822
appear in <i>Creep.</i>

986
00:45:45.923 --> 00:45:47.156
How old are you? 19 or 20?

987
00:45:47.256 --> 00:45:48.758
-Something like that. Okay.
-BARNES: Hmm.

988
00:45:48.859 --> 00:45:50.258
So maybe you know
Lana Del Rey.

989
00:45:50.359 --> 00:45:52.260
who remarkably was sued
by Radiohead

990
00:45:52.361 --> 00:45:55.899
for plagiarizing <i>Creep</i>
in her 2017 song

991
00:45:55.999 --> 00:45:57.365
<i>Get Free.</i>

992
00:45:57.868 --> 00:45:59.235
Iterations.

993
00:45:59.769 --> 00:46:01.070
Over time.

994
00:46:01.905 --> 00:46:03.874
Diluting the message.

995
00:46:03.974 --> 00:46:07.109
Obscuring the original.

996
00:46:09.278 --> 00:46:14.749
Judaism is the OG
monotheistic religion.

997
00:46:14.851 --> 00:46:16.552
It should. by a wide margin.

998
00:46:16.651 --> 00:46:19.488
have the most number of
practicing members. And yet.

999
00:46:19.589 --> 00:46:23.060
it makes up only 0.2%
of the world's population.

1000
00:46:23.159 --> 00:46:24.360
Why is that?

1001
00:46:24.459 --> 00:46:26.762
Why is the original
less popular

1002
